1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,300
The last hundred years has brought us into an astonishing new world of high-tech surveillance.

2
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:23,680
Unmanned sky probes, infrared that sees through walls, tools once used to track our enemies on the battlefield have been turned against private citizens.

3
00:00:23,681 --> 00:00:29,320
Now, spy technology on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The Secret Gathering of Intelligence

5
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:35,000
has been practiced for thousands of years.

6
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:39,000
Many have called it the world's second oldest profession.

7
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:42,000
From weapons of assassination that emit

8
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:44,000
a lethal spray of cyanide gas,

9
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:46,000
and the world's second oldest profession

10
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:50,000
is the world's second oldest profession.

11
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:53,000
Spying, the secret gathering of intelligence

12
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:55,000
has been practiced for thousands of years.

13
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,000
Many have called it the world's second oldest profession.

14
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:04,300
of cyanide gas, to concealed cameras tiny enough to hide

15
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:09,840
in a matchbox, to guns that masquerade as harmless bicycle

16
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:12,440
pumps.

17
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:15,320
Spies have proven themselves to be highly inventive

18
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:18,260
when waging their secret war.

19
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:20,120
But while the human element of spying

20
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:22,560
has remained essentially the same,

21
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:25,300
the tools of the trade have undergone

22
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:26,800
a staggering transformation.

23
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,300
Today, the cutting edge of espionage

24
00:01:32,301 --> 00:01:35,740
relies on every technological breakthrough of the 20th

25
00:01:35,741 --> 00:01:42,920
century, from satellites to lasers to DNA testing.

26
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:47,580
Methods of spying have become so sophisticated and invasive

27
00:01:47,581 --> 00:01:50,100
that some believe they pose a threat to one

28
00:01:50,101 --> 00:01:55,300
of our most precious rights, the right of privacy.

29
00:01:55,301 --> 00:02:01,880
One of the earliest references to espionage

30
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:05,580
comes from The Art of War, written

31
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:11,780
by the Chinese philosopher Sun Tzu more than 2,000 years ago.

32
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:18,360
Sun Tzu wrote, 100 ounces of silver spent for information

33
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:23,220
may save 10,000 spent on war.

34
00:02:23,221 --> 00:02:24,120
spent on war.

35
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:30,160
Over the years, the tricks of the trade

36
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,800
evolved into a codified body of knowledge known

37
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,160
to its practitioners as tradecraft.

38
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:43,280
Tradecraft is the cumulative knowledge of spies that

39
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:46,540
goes back all the way to the Bible, where people

40
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,040
did eavesdropping.

41
00:02:49,041 --> 00:02:55,240
It involves any aspect of your activity as a spy

42
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,740
to obtain information.

43
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:03,860
It can include secret writing, codes, ciphers, surveillance,

44
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:05,360
dead drops.

45
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:09,720
All of these things have been collected and refined over

46
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:13,360
the years by every nation.

47
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:15,800
As far back as the Roman Empire,

48
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,640
military leaders grappled with the question

49
00:03:18,641 --> 00:03:25,340
of how best to transmit messages over vast distances.

50
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,620
If the information was confidential,

51
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,620
emissaries were forced to either memorize it

52
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:36,060
or carry their communication in the form of a coded letter.

53
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:38,600
Code writing or encryption was employed

54
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:42,800
by one of the ancient world's most brilliant tacticians,

55
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:46,200
Julius Caesar.

56
00:03:46,201 --> 00:03:56,220
Julius Caesar's favorite cipher was to simply take the alphabet and move every letter over three.

57
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:04,140
So, for example, if he wanted to write a word that began with the letter A, he would write a D instead of an A.

58
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:09,980
And so B would become E and C would become F and so on.

59
00:04:09,981 --> 00:04:12,580
so that what you would have, if it were written down

60
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:16,620
on a piece of paper, would seem like it was a bunch of nonsense

61
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:19,420
syllables.

62
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:23,220
Early on, individuals schooled in the art of espionage

63
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:27,100
recognized the need to dispatch their enemies with weapons

64
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:29,940
that could be concealed.

65
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:33,640
The infamous Lucrezia Borgia, daughter of a Renaissance

66
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:38,180
pope, was believed to have invented one such assassination

67
00:04:38,181 --> 00:04:40,080
a poison ring.

68
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:46,220
There was a secret compartment that she stored arsenic.

69
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:50,900
And so under the guise of preparing a beverage

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,500
for a visitor, that she could surreptitiously put poison

71
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,760
into the drink and dispatch the visitor.

72
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:04,500
Even in 19th century America,

73
00:05:04,501 --> 00:05:07,740
a society less attuned to the art of spying,

74
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,340
the importance of secret communication

75
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:12,740
was a recognized political tool.

76
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:18,520
When a new cast iron dome was designed for the Capitol

77
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:21,980
building, the architect engineered a whispering spot

78
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:25,620
into its acoustics, making it possible for a whisper

79
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:28,120
to be carried clear across the room.

80
00:05:28,121 --> 00:05:28,821
the room.

81
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:35,120
Through the 19th century, couriers

82
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:37,520
served as the main means of communication.

83
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:43,260
Sometimes they carried secret messages

84
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,840
concealed inside ordinary looking objects,

85
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:51,980
like a silver dollar with a hidden compartment.

86
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:56,420
It was not until the 1840s with the invention of the telegraph

87
00:05:56,421 --> 00:06:04,120
that technology would truly transform the world of spycraft.

88
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:10,560
You no longer had to send the information via word of mouth.

89
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:14,380
You didn't have to have a letter that had to be carried by someone

90
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:19,180
that couldn't travel any faster than the ship or the horse

91
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:21,520
that that person was traveling on.

92
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:24,180
Instead, the use of the telegraph

93
00:06:24,181 --> 00:06:28,280
allowed information to be sent nearly instantaneously

94
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:30,820
across very long distances.

95
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:36,720
During the Civil War, generals from both sides

96
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:40,100
took advantage of the telegraph, using it as a means

97
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:43,240
to relay secret messages.

98
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,660
But both sides soon realized that the miraculous new invention

99
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:48,700
was vulnerable to saboteurs.

100
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:53,380
You cut the telegraph wire, and you've

101
00:06:53,381 --> 00:06:57,560
and you've severed that instantaneous means of communication.

102
00:06:57,561 --> 00:07:04,620
And so along with sabotaging the railroads and roads

103
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:10,060
and bridges in general, it became part of warfare

104
00:07:10,061 --> 00:07:14,060
to also cut the lines of communication, in this case,

105
00:07:14,061 --> 00:07:15,900
the telegraph lines of communication.

106
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,040
As the war raged on between North and South,

107
00:07:23,041 --> 00:07:25,580
the need for the visual observation of the enemy

108
00:07:25,581 --> 00:07:27,080
became ever more apparent.

109
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:33,120
Military tacticians had long been aware of the value

110
00:07:33,121 --> 00:07:34,880
of taking the high ground.

111
00:07:34,881 --> 00:07:37,480
And it was not long before the war became the stage

112
00:07:37,481 --> 00:07:41,400
for another innovative idea, aerial reconnaissance.

113
00:07:44,460 --> 00:07:47,160
There was a gentleman named Thaddeus Lowe

114
00:07:47,161 --> 00:07:48,960
who brought his balloons in.

115
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:52,400
And they were used in several battles around Manassas.

116
00:07:52,401 --> 00:07:55,460
The Confederate troops had moved very close to Washington.

117
00:07:55,461 --> 00:07:57,900
In fact, they were just across the river.

118
00:07:57,901 --> 00:08:01,500
So Thaddeus Lowe would go up in his balloon

119
00:08:01,501 --> 00:08:04,840
and then make a note of where the troops were.

120
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:10,220
Union commanders were soon sending up balloons

121
00:08:10,221 --> 00:08:12,360
so topographers could produce maps.

122
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:20,160
By the turn of the century, industrialization

123
00:08:20,161 --> 00:08:25,560
advances were having a dramatic impact on the way people lived.

124
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:29,340
These forces would have an equally powerful effect on how

125
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:33,440
people fought wars and spied on one another.

126
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:42,340
By 1914, with the outbreak of World War I,

127
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:45,920
the major European powers began using airplanes

128
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:47,480
for aerial reconnaissance.

129
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:53,260
French aircraft were equipped with cameras that could take

130
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:57,700
clear pictures from as high as 3,000 feet.

131
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:00,640
Photographs of the battlefield served as the basis

132
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:03,480
for detailed maps of the enemy's deployments.

133
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:16,180
Combatants also recognized the potential of the telegraph

134
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:18,080
for military planning.

135
00:09:18,081 --> 00:09:20,320
English saboteurs were quick to go to work,

136
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:22,820
cutting Germany's underwater cable to Finland.

137
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:31,560
Spies were forced to find alternative methods

138
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:34,100
for passing information, sometimes

139
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:39,300
stuffing messages inside cigars or using invisible ink

140
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,200
concealed in a talcum powder can.

141
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:46,900
Soon, military commanders and secret agents from Air Force

142
00:09:46,901 --> 00:09:50,440
secret agents from every nation were availing themselves of a

143
00:09:50,441 --> 00:09:53,800
much more powerful tool, the radio.

144
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,400
With the advent of wireless, you no longer had to be at a

145
00:10:01,401 --> 00:10:05,840
fixed point at one end of a cable communicating to somebody

146
00:10:05,841 --> 00:10:08,660
else at the fixed point at another end of the cable.

147
00:10:08,661 --> 00:10:14,540
You could establish small transmitting stations wherever

148
00:10:14,541 --> 00:10:15,640
you needed to be.

149
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:19,940
Each nation erected antennas for the receipt

150
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:22,600
of its own communications.

151
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:25,280
More often than not, enemy transmissions

152
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:28,880
were intercepted as well.

153
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:32,140
As public airways were being used for the most highly

154
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:35,580
confidential communications, it soon became clear

155
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:39,720
that more elaborate codes were needed.

156
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:42,520
As long as you are sending your message

157
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:46,900
in some form of code, it doesn't matter how much

158
00:10:46,901 --> 00:10:51,300
many people read it as long as they don't have the key to the code.

159
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:57,040
Often the first line of a well-known novel

160
00:10:57,140 --> 00:11:00,220
served as the key that would unlock the meaning of what

161
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:03,960
appeared to be merely garbled syllables.

162
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:06,180
Let's take Dickens, A Tale of Two Cities.

163
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:10,120
You know, famous first line, it was the best of times,

164
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:12,220
it was the worst of times.

165
00:11:12,221 --> 00:11:19,260
And above those lines, you write the alphabet in order, A, B, C, D, E, F, G.

166
00:11:19,261 --> 00:11:27,620
And so you now have a key that lets you know how to transform your alphabet,

167
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:35,640
how to encode the alphabet in a way that is only going to be decipherable to people who know

168
00:11:35,641 --> 00:11:38,520
that they're supposed to look at the first line of A Tale of Two Cities.

169
00:11:38,521 --> 00:11:43,520
Perhaps the greatest intelligence coup of World War

170
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:48,160
I came in 1917 with the interception and decoding

171
00:11:48,260 --> 00:11:49,860
of the Zimmerman telegram.

172
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:55,400
The gist of the telegram was a proposal sent

173
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:59,180
by the German foreign minister to the Mexican embassy,

174
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:02,220
promising that if Mexico joined in an alliance

175
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:04,820
against the United States, Mexico

176
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:07,740
would be greatly rewarded by the return of the law

177
00:12:07,741 --> 00:12:11,640
of the lost territories of New Mexico, Arizona,

178
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:12,740
and portions of Texas.

179
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:18,880
By being able to decipher that telegram

180
00:12:18,980 --> 00:12:23,420
and make its contents known to the United States,

181
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:28,000
the British were able to probably advance the date that

182
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:31,500
the United States joined the war and bring to an end

183
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:35,500
what was a very ugly and costly stalemate in Europe

184
00:12:35,501 --> 00:12:36,740
in Europe at that time.

185
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:43,200
With the end of the war, espionage receded

186
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:45,340
into the background.

187
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:48,540
In the United States, an attitude that spying was

188
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:51,340
un-American prevailed.

189
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:54,760
Proclaiming the gentlemen did not read each other's mail,

190
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:57,560
Henry Stimson, the then Secretary of State,

191
00:12:57,660 --> 00:13:01,620
disbanded the only organization dedicated to the job of signals

192
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:05,100
intercept and code breaking.

193
00:13:05,101 --> 00:13:08,440
It would take the threat of another global conflict

194
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:12,940
before the United States would call its spies back into action.

195
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:23,380
A dead drop is a prearranged location

196
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:26,360
at which spies can leave information or collect

197
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:27,760
instructions.

198
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:29,960
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

199
00:13:29,961 --> 00:13:38,460
We now return to Spy Technology on Modern Models.

200
00:13:41,060 --> 00:13:51,020
By the mid-1930s, Hitler's willingness to brush aside the limitations imposed by the Versailles Treaty was becoming alarmingly clear.

201
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:57,960
British and French spies attempted to assess Germany's military might.

202
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:04,800
Secret agents developed cunning new gadgets,

203
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:10,000
like a miniature camera, tiny enough to fit into a matchbox,

204
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:14,000
yet perfectly designed for copying documents.

205
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:25,520
But when in 1939 German tanks rolled across Czechoslovakia,

206
00:14:25,620 --> 00:14:28,320
it was apparent how woefully intelligence

207
00:14:28,321 --> 00:14:31,820
intelligence had underestimated Hitler's capabilities.

208
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:39,940
Once full-scale fighting erupted, espionage

209
00:14:39,941 --> 00:14:42,840
efforts grew dramatically.

210
00:14:42,841 --> 00:14:46,480
Radio transmission afforded the secret agent a mobility

211
00:14:46,481 --> 00:14:49,780
heretofore unknown.

212
00:14:49,781 --> 00:14:53,180
One of the things that allowed for the success

213
00:14:53,181 --> 00:14:56,440
of the Maquis, the French underground,

214
00:14:56,441 --> 00:15:01,740
and the Polish underground who fought against the Germans

215
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:05,040
from within German-occupied territory

216
00:15:05,140 --> 00:15:08,580
was the fact that there were small,

217
00:15:08,680 --> 00:15:12,620
suitcase-sized radio transmitters

218
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:14,960
that could be carried from site to site,

219
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:18,760
a different site, every night.

220
00:15:18,860 --> 00:15:21,700
Secret communications flooded the air.

221
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:24,060
The only drawback was that these signals

222
00:15:24,061 --> 00:15:25,760
could be easily intercepted.

223
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:30,500
In order to meet this problem head on,

224
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:35,500
some messages were encoded and then broadcast on BBC radio

225
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:38,000
during the nightly newscasts over Europe.

226
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:44,920
The BBC would include little cryptic messages

227
00:15:45,020 --> 00:15:49,260
like Marie Claire's kitten is lapping milk,

228
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:52,720
something that would make absolutely no sense

229
00:15:52,721 --> 00:15:55,820
to most people who were listening,

230
00:15:55,920 --> 00:16:01,100
but to a specific group of partisans

231
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:04,240
would let them know that one of their members,

232
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:07,840
who had had to be spirited out of occupied France,

233
00:16:07,940 --> 00:16:10,540
that she had arrived safely in England.

234
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:15,480
Agents working behind enemy lines

235
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:18,180
used a wide range of specialized weapons

236
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:21,580
that could be concealed.

237
00:16:21,581 --> 00:16:24,320
A double-edged knife that could be sewn into the lapel

238
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:27,020
of a jacket.

239
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:31,420
A deadly two-inch steel dart adapted to fit into the nose

240
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:35,120
of a Colt .45 and fired at close range.

241
00:16:39,940 --> 00:16:43,440
And the well rod pistol, complete with silencer that

242
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:45,840
looked as innocuous as a bicycle pump.

243
00:16:51,580 --> 00:16:54,480
The biggest challenge for Allied intelligence

244
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:58,780
was a German encryption machine called the Enigma,

245
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:00,960
designed to automatically generate

246
00:17:01,060 --> 00:17:05,320
new and highly complex codes up to three times a day.

247
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:10,840
In order to crack the German cipher,

248
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:13,700
the British war office assembled a brain trust

249
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:15,040
of their top scientists.

250
00:17:15,041 --> 00:17:15,940
scientists.

251
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:23,280
Housed at Bletchley Park, a Victorian mansion 50 miles

252
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:25,480
north of London, these men and women

253
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:28,520
worked against the clock.

254
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:31,280
Among them was a young mathematician named Alan

255
00:17:31,380 --> 00:17:32,700
Turing.

256
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:38,360
Turing deduced the theoretical principles behind the enigma.

257
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:41,900
In 1943, over a period of nine months,

258
00:17:41,901 --> 00:17:45,440
Bletchley engineers worked to his specifications,

259
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:50,040
building a code-breaking machine called the Colossus, which

260
00:17:50,140 --> 00:17:55,040
is believed to be the very first mechanical computer.

261
00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:58,380
The Colossus enabled the British to rapidly decipher messages

262
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:01,720
between Hitler and his generals.

263
00:18:01,820 --> 00:18:04,220
It was necessary, however, to keep their triumph

264
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:07,120
a well-guarded secret.

265
00:18:07,220 --> 00:18:10,420
Often, the British were unable to mount visible evacuation

266
00:18:10,421 --> 00:18:15,260
efforts because doing so would tip their hand.

267
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:18,820
It really presented some horrible ethical dilemmas

268
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:24,500
about sacrificing some in the short term with the hope

269
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:28,340
that in the long term many, many more could be saved.

270
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:33,440
Many lives were also saved by advances

271
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:36,180
in aerial photography.

272
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:38,480
Allied bombers equipped with cameras

273
00:18:38,481 --> 00:18:42,120
were active on the European front.

274
00:18:42,220 --> 00:18:45,620
Aerial photographs proved invaluable in providing

275
00:18:45,720 --> 00:18:49,120
information about enemy troop movements and bomb damage

276
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:50,220
assessment reports.

277
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:59,660
During World War II, intelligence sources,

278
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:03,680
ranging from spies, communications intercepts,

279
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:08,680
and aerial photos produced a flood of information.

280
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:10,980
It was now clear that the outcome of the war

281
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:14,600
depended on far more than military might.

282
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:18,060
Espionage had emerged as a powerful new weapon.

283
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:25,440
And once the fighting ended, the intelligence community,

284
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:29,480
now a force on its own, would no longer

285
00:19:29,580 --> 00:19:33,680
be content to recede into the background.

286
00:19:33,681 --> 00:19:47,560
During the war, the real-life view of James Bond fame devised weapons that could be concealed

287
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:51,320
in everyday objects, such as hairbrushes and pens.

288
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,340
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

289
00:19:58,340 --> 00:20:03,440
We now return to Spy Technology on Modern Marvels.

290
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:12,060
In 1945, the United States brought to an end

291
00:20:12,160 --> 00:20:16,620
a decade of bloodshed by dropping the atomic bomb.

292
00:20:19,800 --> 00:20:22,660
Americans everywhere hoped their hard-won victory

293
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:24,460
would usher in an era of peace.

294
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:30,080
Instead, the nation found itself confronted

295
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:33,380
with a sinister new enemy, an enemy

296
00:20:33,381 --> 00:20:38,720
that stood for the brutal suppression of freedom.

297
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:40,920
Stopping the spread of communism was soon

298
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,420
a national obsession.

299
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:47,160
Paranoia and suspicion festered as Western leaders

300
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:54,360
tried to puzzle out Stalin's intentions and capabilities.

301
00:20:54,460 --> 00:20:56,900
But what made Russia all the more menacing

302
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,340
was the veil of secrecy that surrounded it.

303
00:21:00,341 --> 00:21:03,240
Soviet society was so secretive,

304
00:21:03,241 --> 00:21:08,020
even the Moscow phone book was classified.

305
00:21:08,021 --> 00:21:13,220
Winston Churchill had a unique way of catching a word

306
00:21:13,221 --> 00:21:16,960
or a phrase and typifying the times.

307
00:21:16,961 --> 00:21:21,060
An iron curtain has descended across the continent.

308
00:21:21,061 --> 00:21:24,960
Behind that line lie all the capitals

309
00:21:24,961 --> 00:21:28,960
of the ancient states of Central and Eastern Europe.

310
00:21:28,961 --> 00:21:35,100
The Soviets had literally established a curtain

311
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:38,540
that was impenetrable from the ground.

312
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:40,500
You couldn't get through it.

313
00:21:40,600 --> 00:21:45,440
And it was the iron curtain that was the opening shot

314
00:21:45,540 --> 00:21:48,120
of the Cold War.

315
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:53,860
Then, in 1949, Russia tested its own atomic bomb

316
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:55,020
and upped the ante.

317
00:21:55,021 --> 00:22:00,020
The Cold War became a war like none before it,

318
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:04,500
an armed truce maintained through feint and bluff.

319
00:22:04,600 --> 00:22:08,060
Its soldiers came not from the armed forces,

320
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:12,200
but from a small cadre of specially trained secret agents

321
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:16,140
called the Central Intelligence Agency.

322
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:22,080
They faced a formidable adversary in the Russian KGB.

323
00:22:22,081 --> 00:22:25,680
Both sides designed new weapons of espionage.

324
00:22:27,820 --> 00:22:32,900
The microdot, a tiny photograph of a secret document so small

325
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:36,020
that it could only be read with the aid of a high-powered

326
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:40,940
magnifying device, yet so handy that a full page of text

327
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:46,500
could be compressed into an area the size of a pinprick.

328
00:22:46,600 --> 00:22:52,080
A cyanide gas gun, a much feared assassination device.

329
00:22:52,081 --> 00:22:56,080
designed to emit a silent spray of poison gas

330
00:22:56,180 --> 00:23:00,020
and capable of causing instant death when fired directly

331
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:01,220
into a victim's face.

332
00:23:04,920 --> 00:23:08,500
The Minox, an ultra miniature precision camera

333
00:23:08,600 --> 00:23:12,140
with a focusing range of eight inches to infinity,

334
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:14,800
so ideal for clandestine photography

335
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:17,340
that it became the workhorse of the Cold War.

336
00:23:17,341 --> 00:23:22,780
Yet despite these advances, US attempts to pierce the Iron

337
00:23:22,781 --> 00:23:25,580
Curtain failed.

338
00:23:25,581 --> 00:23:28,880
But there was no way to infiltrate.

339
00:23:28,881 --> 00:23:31,580
The US government and Central Intelligence Agency

340
00:23:31,581 --> 00:23:35,600
tried to parachute people in, tried to secretly land them

341
00:23:35,601 --> 00:23:37,600
in, get them in by boat.

342
00:23:37,601 --> 00:23:39,500
None of these things worked.

343
00:23:39,501 --> 00:23:41,600
Tradecraft had fallen down.

344
00:23:41,601 --> 00:23:43,240
It wasn't working.

345
00:23:43,241 --> 00:23:47,340
And the need for a new war was to be able to get

346
00:23:47,341 --> 00:23:52,480
knowledge of the adversary was great.

347
00:23:52,580 --> 00:23:56,080
In 1954, military advisors informed

348
00:23:56,180 --> 00:23:59,780
President Dwight D. Eisenhower that the Soviets were building

349
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:04,860
a new long-range bomber called the Bison.

350
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:08,120
They warned that the Bison could carry a nuclear warhead

351
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:11,760
all the way to America.

352
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:15,960
We're living under the specter that at any time

353
00:24:15,961 --> 00:24:20,640
Russia could drop an atomic bomb on us, burst our balloon, end our party.

354
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:25,740
It's the era of the Strategic Air Command, always having planes in the air.

355
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:32,780
Our defenses have to be manned all the time because we know the Russians can attack it anytime.

356
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:36,540
And we grow up under that specter.

357
00:24:37,060 --> 00:24:43,360
But yet we continue to function because we know we can always launch the retaliatory strike.

358
00:24:43,361 --> 00:24:47,060
Eisenhower believed the only way to reduce the tensions

359
00:24:47,160 --> 00:24:51,100
of the Cold War was to prove there was no bomber gap.

360
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:01,940
His experience in World War II had taught him the value

361
00:25:02,040 --> 00:25:08,420
of aerial reconnaissance, yet he felt reluctant to use it.

362
00:25:08,421 --> 00:25:12,420
Flying an airplane over a possible adversary

363
00:25:12,520 --> 00:25:14,020
really concerned him.

364
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:18,160
He thought this might be the beginning of World War III.

365
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:20,460
He knew that we needed information,

366
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:24,500
but he was really reluctant to do such a daring thing.

367
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:29,480
Deciding the gains outweighed the risks.

368
00:25:29,580 --> 00:25:31,880
Eisenhower green-lighted the project.

369
00:25:31,881 --> 00:25:32,680
the project.

370
00:25:36,280 --> 00:25:40,020
Designed by Clarence Kelly Johnson at Lockheed Skunk Works

371
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:42,620
facility on the California desert,

372
00:25:42,720 --> 00:25:45,120
the plane was called the U-2.

373
00:25:45,220 --> 00:25:49,540
It was engineered to fly at an altitude of 70,000 feet,

374
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:53,140
beyond the reach of radar detection.

375
00:25:53,240 --> 00:25:58,140
It flew so high, in fact, that from the first flights of the U-2,

376
00:25:58,141 --> 00:26:03,140
We got this tremendous rash of reports of UFOs.

377
00:26:03,240 --> 00:26:06,640
No one thought that an aircraft could fly that high.

378
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:10,380
No, the problems to be solved to fly that high are enormous.

379
00:26:10,480 --> 00:26:13,180
To keep the pilot alive was enormous.

380
00:26:13,280 --> 00:26:15,900
U-2 pilots were the first astronauts.

381
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:20,800
They had to be in suits that would keep them alive,

382
00:26:20,900 --> 00:26:24,800
because if they didn't have a pressure suit on,

383
00:26:24,900 --> 00:26:26,900
they would die, their blood would boil.

384
00:26:26,901 --> 00:26:30,000
Well, they're at the very edge of the stratosphere.

385
00:26:32,340 --> 00:26:35,380
From 13 miles high, the U-2's camera

386
00:26:35,480 --> 00:26:39,620
captured a view of the Earth unlike any other.

387
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:42,740
It could bring into focus objects as small as 2 and 1

388
00:26:42,840 --> 00:26:43,820
half feet across.

389
00:26:46,780 --> 00:26:49,920
In 1955, Eisenhower traveled to Geneva

390
00:26:50,020 --> 00:26:53,620
for his first meeting with Soviet leaders.

391
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:56,880
Hoping to avoid the risk of violating Soviet air

392
00:26:56,900 --> 00:27:00,240
he made a daring proposal.

393
00:27:00,340 --> 00:27:02,740
Called the Open Skies Initiative, it

394
00:27:02,840 --> 00:27:05,880
would allow both sides to see if the other was preparing

395
00:27:05,980 --> 00:27:08,440
to attack.

396
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:09,780
The plan was rejected.

397
00:27:12,920 --> 00:27:16,660
For Eisenhower, the time had come to send the U-2 out

398
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:20,260
over Soviet territory.

399
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:23,200
The film that was returned was rushed to a small group

400
00:27:23,300 --> 00:27:26,900
of experts for interpretation.

401
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:31,900
What we were looking for was a Bison bomber.

402
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,700
The Bison was an intercontinental bomber.

403
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:38,440
Supposedly the Soviets had a lot more bombers than we did.

404
00:27:38,540 --> 00:27:41,440
So we flew right over Moscow, Leningrad,

405
00:27:41,540 --> 00:27:44,960
and we flew right over their long-range bomber airfields.

406
00:27:45,060 --> 00:27:48,060
Within three months, we could go to President Eisenhower

407
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:50,360
and say, Mr. President, there's no bomber gap.

408
00:27:53,960 --> 00:27:56,880
The U-2 photos proved what all the

409
00:27:56,900 --> 00:28:00,000
Eisenhower had suspected all along,

410
00:28:00,001 --> 00:28:03,380
the Russians had exaggerated their military might.

411
00:28:03,381 --> 00:28:05,740
But this was not to be the end of his conflict

412
00:28:05,741 --> 00:28:07,680
with his advisors.

413
00:28:07,681 --> 00:28:10,920
The Air Force now insisted Russia was deploying hundreds

414
00:28:10,921 --> 00:28:13,280
of intercontinental ballistic missiles.

415
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:19,820
By the spring of 1960, Eisenhower managed

416
00:28:19,821 --> 00:28:24,020
to arrange a summit with Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev,

417
00:28:24,021 --> 00:28:26,420
with nuclear disarmament as his agenda.

418
00:28:29,860 --> 00:28:32,660
Believing he needed to know more about Russia's supposed

419
00:28:32,661 --> 00:28:37,340
stockpile of ICBMs, Eisenhower approved one last flight

420
00:28:37,341 --> 00:28:40,500
of the U-2.

421
00:28:40,501 --> 00:28:44,680
But the U-2 piloted by Francis Gary Powers was spotted

422
00:28:44,681 --> 00:28:48,540
and shot down over northern Russia.

423
00:28:48,541 --> 00:28:50,840
Remarkably, Powers survived.

424
00:28:50,841 --> 00:28:56,420
But both he and his role of film fell into Soviet hands.

425
00:28:56,421 --> 00:28:59,880
An outrage, Khrushchev canceled the summit,

426
00:28:59,881 --> 00:29:03,680
crushing Eisenhower's hopes for nuclear disarmament

427
00:29:03,681 --> 00:29:10,080
and ending his dream of leaving a lasting legacy of peace.

428
00:29:10,081 --> 00:29:14,080
Eisenhower was forced to stop all U-2 overflights.

429
00:29:14,081 --> 00:29:17,160
Three months later, Powers was tried as a spy

430
00:29:17,161 --> 00:29:21,540
and sentenced to 10 years in a Siberian prison.

431
00:29:21,541 --> 00:29:26,240
Ironically, on the very same day, a headline ran in the papers

432
00:29:26,241 --> 00:29:30,540
announcing the successful launch of the Discover 14 satellite.

433
00:29:33,980 --> 00:29:37,720
But no one realized was that the Discoverer was a front

434
00:29:37,721 --> 00:29:39,940
for a top secret project.

435
00:29:39,941 --> 00:29:44,280
And the United States once again had a spy in the sky.

436
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:56,220
In the 1950s, the Soviets planted a listening device in the American embassy in Moscow,

437
00:29:56,880 --> 00:29:59,080
directly over the ambassador's desk.

438
00:29:59,740 --> 00:30:04,180
It went undetected for years, eavesdropping on his conversations.

439
00:30:05,060 --> 00:30:07,200
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

440
00:30:11,380 --> 00:30:16,600
We now return to Spy Technology on Modern Marvels.

441
00:30:18,160 --> 00:30:20,660
In the late 1950s, a space project

442
00:30:20,760 --> 00:30:23,700
was authorized that in the years to come,

443
00:30:23,800 --> 00:30:28,100
would transform intelligence gathering.

444
00:30:28,200 --> 00:30:31,600
Built as a space exploration program that could carry

445
00:30:31,700 --> 00:30:34,920
monkeys into orbit, the Discoverer satellite

446
00:30:35,020 --> 00:30:37,220
employed hundreds of technicians

447
00:30:37,320 --> 00:30:39,020
and was the darling of the press.

448
00:30:39,021 --> 00:30:39,920
press.

449
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:49,360
But unbeknownst to all but a few, Discoverer

450
00:30:49,460 --> 00:30:54,360
was a cover story for Corona, the world's first spy satellite.

451
00:30:57,980 --> 00:31:00,580
Begun on the Vandenberg Air Force Base,

452
00:31:00,680 --> 00:31:02,280
the project was plagued by failures.

453
00:31:09,020 --> 00:31:10,780
After repeated launch attempts,

454
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:16,960
Discoverer 14 finally succeeded in putting a camera into space.

455
00:31:17,060 --> 00:31:20,160
The satellite orbited the Earth 17 times,

456
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:23,260
then ejected a capsule filled with exposed film.

457
00:31:29,780 --> 00:31:32,880
Once recovered, the film was delivered to analysts,

458
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:37,280
who examined the images of more than 1.5 million square miles

459
00:31:37,281 --> 00:31:41,080
of Soviet territory.

460
00:31:41,180 --> 00:31:46,180
Supposedly, the Soviets were going to have 250 missile sites,

461
00:31:46,280 --> 00:31:48,420
and we only found 15.

462
00:31:48,520 --> 00:31:51,320
We took great delight in doing this,

463
00:31:51,420 --> 00:31:53,520
but you can imagine if you were in the Defense Department

464
00:31:53,620 --> 00:31:56,840
and all of a sudden here was this little organization saying,

465
00:31:56,940 --> 00:31:59,140
you know, that's a lie.

466
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:04,740
Well, we weren't that popular, except with the president.

467
00:32:04,741 --> 00:32:09,040
In 1960, a new president who had campaigned on a hard line,

468
00:32:09,140 --> 00:32:13,440
anti-communist ticket took office.

469
00:32:13,540 --> 00:32:15,680
Having denounced the previous administration

470
00:32:15,780 --> 00:32:18,920
for allowing Russia to take the lead in the arms race,

471
00:32:19,020 --> 00:32:22,420
John F. Kennedy pushed for a major increase in defense

472
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:24,060
spending.

473
00:32:24,160 --> 00:32:28,360
But knowing that here on Earth, God's work

474
00:32:28,460 --> 00:32:31,800
must truly be our own.

475
00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:33,940
It was no coincidence that Kennedy

476
00:32:33,941 --> 00:32:36,940
called the James Bond thriller from Russia with Love

477
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:42,040
his favorite movie, especially touting its high-tech antics.

478
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:47,980
Ian Fleming continued this notion

479
00:32:48,080 --> 00:32:52,500
of the fight of absolute good as represented

480
00:32:52,600 --> 00:32:59,000
by Anglo-American technology, cultural values,

481
00:32:59,100 --> 00:33:03,000
against absolute evil.

482
00:33:03,001 --> 00:33:10,600
And evil may take the form of Dr. No instead of the evil empire

483
00:33:10,700 --> 00:33:12,740
of the Soviet Union.

484
00:33:12,840 --> 00:33:16,640
In reality, Cold War spies were availing themselves

485
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:19,140
of the new and secret weapons of death.

486
00:33:21,260 --> 00:33:24,360
Many of these seemed straight out of a James Bond thriller.

487
00:33:27,660 --> 00:33:31,900
A CIA stinger, a reloadable .22 caliber firing device

488
00:33:31,901 --> 00:33:33,600
concealed in a paint tube.

489
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:39,640
A poison-tipped umbrella, when pressed against the skin

490
00:33:39,740 --> 00:33:42,240
of a victim, an apparatus in its tip

491
00:33:42,340 --> 00:33:45,540
injected a pellet of fatal poison into the body.

492
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:54,660
A 4.5 millimeter gun sheathed in a rubber case,

493
00:33:54,760 --> 00:33:59,060
designed to fire by twisting the barrel a quarter turn

494
00:33:59,061 --> 00:34:01,700
and small enough to conceal in the rectum.

495
00:34:10,040 --> 00:34:13,080
As the struggle between the superpowers intensified,

496
00:34:13,180 --> 00:34:16,440
the gadgets of the spy trade were replaced by a far more

497
00:34:16,540 --> 00:34:18,040
deadly array of weapons.

498
00:34:21,180 --> 00:34:23,780
The crisis began in the summer of 1960,

499
00:34:23,880 --> 00:34:25,880
when a communist revolution in Cuba

500
00:34:25,881 --> 00:34:30,880
provided the Soviets with a strategic foothold only 90 miles

501
00:34:30,980 --> 00:34:32,420
from the Florida coast.

502
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:37,660
Because of slow turnaround time,

503
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:41,700
corona photographs were of little use in the crisis.

504
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:46,260
Once again, the U-2 was called into action.

505
00:34:46,360 --> 00:34:49,240
Well, that's when we found the missiles, the medium range

506
00:34:49,340 --> 00:34:51,040
ballistic missile sites.

507
00:34:51,140 --> 00:34:52,240
They were unique.

508
00:34:52,340 --> 00:34:55,380
The first thing is if you had a 100-foot tent,

509
00:34:55,381 --> 00:34:57,720
You had 65-foot trailers.

510
00:34:57,820 --> 00:35:00,620
You had tents for military personnel.

511
00:35:00,720 --> 00:35:02,680
And it was obvious the way these tents were laid out.

512
00:35:02,780 --> 00:35:03,880
It was military.

513
00:35:03,980 --> 00:35:08,120
It wasn't a Boy Scout jamboree or anything like that.

514
00:35:08,220 --> 00:35:10,420
By the next day, photo interpreters

515
00:35:10,520 --> 00:35:13,040
briefed the president, pointing out

516
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:18,540
that intermediate-range missile sites were under construction.

517
00:35:18,640 --> 00:35:21,380
The photography was really driving the crisis.

518
00:35:21,480 --> 00:35:24,040
We were seeing things on the photography

519
00:35:24,041 --> 00:35:26,880
that was being briefed to the president and the executive

520
00:35:26,980 --> 00:35:30,880
committee, making them make decisions that they had to do

521
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:32,540
something and do something fast.

522
00:35:32,640 --> 00:35:36,080
Within the past week, unmistakable evidence

523
00:35:36,180 --> 00:35:40,560
has established the fact that a series of offensive missile

524
00:35:40,660 --> 00:35:46,000
sites is now in preparation on that imprisoned island.

525
00:35:46,100 --> 00:35:50,700
The purpose of these bases can be none other than to provide

526
00:35:50,701 --> 00:35:54,700
a nuclear strike capability against the Western Hemisphere.

527
00:35:54,701 --> 00:35:59,700
Around noon, we get word that a U-2 has been shot down.

528
00:35:59,701 --> 00:36:04,700
That afternoon, we get word that they're firing at our low-altitude planes.

529
00:36:04,701 --> 00:36:07,700
Khrushchev hadn't been seen for three days,

530
00:36:07,701 --> 00:36:11,700
and they were digging trenches all over the island.

531
00:36:11,701 --> 00:36:17,700
Top military advisors urged Kennedy to invade Cuba.

532
00:36:17,701 --> 00:36:20,800
Kennedy took a middle course and ordered a naval blockade

533
00:36:20,900 --> 00:36:22,540
instead.

534
00:36:22,640 --> 00:36:25,600
Aware from the corona photos that in a confrontation,

535
00:36:25,700 --> 00:36:28,980
the US had the more extensive nuclear arsenal,

536
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:32,040
he held his ground.

537
00:36:32,140 --> 00:36:34,380
Finally, the Soviets backed down.

538
00:36:37,660 --> 00:36:39,560
Once again, high-tech surveillance

539
00:36:39,660 --> 00:36:44,060
helped sidestep a nuclear showdown.

540
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:46,600
Over the years, as US intelligence gathering

541
00:36:46,601 --> 00:36:50,200
capabilities have become ever more sophisticated.

542
00:36:50,300 --> 00:36:53,300
Some troubling questions have emerged,

543
00:36:53,400 --> 00:36:57,140
ones that have led experts to warn that our personal liberties

544
00:36:57,240 --> 00:36:58,240
may be in danger.

545
00:37:02,280 --> 00:37:05,820
The Russian KGB, the Committee for State Security,

546
00:37:05,920 --> 00:37:08,320
was responsible for foreign intelligence

547
00:37:08,420 --> 00:37:12,960
until it was disbanded in 1991 at the demise of the Soviet

548
00:37:13,060 --> 00:37:14,100
Union.

549
00:37:14,101 --> 00:37:16,340
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

550
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:25,840
We now return to spy technology on Modern Marvels.

551
00:37:28,440 --> 00:37:31,320
For nearly four decades, spies up above

552
00:37:31,420 --> 00:37:35,720
have monitored fast-breaking world events.

553
00:37:35,820 --> 00:37:39,400
Although the corona was retired in 1972,

554
00:37:39,500 --> 00:37:43,300
it has been followed by several more sophisticated systems,

555
00:37:43,301 --> 00:37:45,300
all of which remain classified.

556
00:37:50,400 --> 00:37:52,200
The current state-of-the-art satellite

557
00:37:52,300 --> 00:37:55,840
is said to be the advanced KH-11, believed to have been

558
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:57,040
launched from the shuttle.

559
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:02,580
While details of the satellite's operating system

560
00:38:02,680 --> 00:38:06,420
are largely unknown, the KH-11's capabilities

561
00:38:06,520 --> 00:38:10,260
are thought to be similar to the Hubble Space Telescope, which

562
00:38:10,261 --> 00:38:14,500
which delivers real-time digital imagery via coded signals

563
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:15,300
to Earth.

564
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:23,540
From 200 miles out in space, the KH-11, like the Hubble,

565
00:38:23,640 --> 00:38:28,580
can bring into focus objects the size of a grapefruit.

566
00:38:28,680 --> 00:38:31,340
It also possesses infrared capabilities,

567
00:38:31,440 --> 00:38:34,120
which allow it to take pictures in complete darkness.

568
00:38:34,121 --> 00:38:42,220
The United States relied on the KH-11 and two other cutting edge

569
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:44,400
imaging systems during the Gulf War.

570
00:38:46,940 --> 00:38:50,240
But satellites not only function as the eyes of the countries

571
00:38:50,340 --> 00:38:54,680
that own them, they also act as the ears, listening in

572
00:38:54,780 --> 00:38:58,540
on all sorts of communications.

573
00:38:58,640 --> 00:39:00,720
These communications are monitored

574
00:39:00,721 --> 00:39:03,860
are monitored by the most secretive spy agency

575
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:10,060
in the United States, the National Security Agency.

576
00:39:10,160 --> 00:39:13,560
It has an array of ground stations around the world,

577
00:39:13,660 --> 00:39:16,000
with a variety of listening antenna.

578
00:39:16,100 --> 00:39:19,440
It has ships equipped with eavesdropping equipment.

579
00:39:19,540 --> 00:39:23,940
It has aircraft outfitted with a huge array of antennas,

580
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:27,820
computers, and so forth to fly near target areas.

581
00:39:27,920 --> 00:39:30,120
And it has a constellation of satellites

582
00:39:30,121 --> 00:39:33,220
that are also capable of eavesdropping.

583
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:37,220
The NSA intercepts all sorts of communications

584
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:40,840
transmitted by everything from microwave telephone

585
00:39:40,940 --> 00:39:43,900
to walkie-talkie to electronic signals.

586
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:50,780
The flow is so overwhelming and constant

587
00:39:50,880 --> 00:39:52,980
that even with the aid of computers,

588
00:39:53,080 --> 00:39:56,820
analysts are able to monitor and process only a small fraction.

589
00:40:00,120 --> 00:40:03,720
But even the most comprehensive data beamed back by a satellite

590
00:40:03,820 --> 00:40:05,960
cannot answer the crucial question

591
00:40:06,060 --> 00:40:10,460
at the heart of every crisis.

592
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:13,500
You don't know what's in the minds of the leaders.

593
00:40:13,600 --> 00:40:16,880
You don't know if somebody is irrational.

594
00:40:16,980 --> 00:40:20,240
You might know about a nuclear capability.

595
00:40:20,340 --> 00:40:21,780
You might know where bases are.

596
00:40:21,880 --> 00:40:24,440
But you don't know the character of the leadership.

597
00:40:27,540 --> 00:40:29,240
You may think you know it.

598
00:40:29,241 --> 00:40:32,880
I mean, ultimately, it's human beings who are making

599
00:40:32,980 --> 00:40:38,180
the decisions, and that's the intangible.

600
00:40:38,280 --> 00:40:40,760
Today, it is believed that the United States

601
00:40:40,860 --> 00:40:45,420
has up to 40 satellite watchdogs in the sky.

602
00:40:45,520 --> 00:40:47,620
The exact number worldwide is unknown.

603
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:52,360
With the collapse of the Soviet Union

604
00:40:52,460 --> 00:40:55,140
and the end of the Cold War, policymakers

605
00:40:55,240 --> 00:40:59,240
say that the world has become an even more dangerous place.

606
00:40:59,241 --> 00:41:06,240
They admit that they no longer know where the next threat will come from.

607
00:41:06,241 --> 00:41:11,240
Terrorism is the greatest challenge for intelligence today.

608
00:41:11,241 --> 00:41:14,240
It's a challenge because it's so small.

609
00:41:14,241 --> 00:41:18,240
You know, you have small groups that can do a great deal of damage.

610
00:41:18,241 --> 00:41:23,240
You know, there weren't that many people involved in the big bombing in the World Trade Center in New York.

611
00:41:23,241 --> 00:41:24,240
You only have eight or ten people.

612
00:41:24,241 --> 00:41:25,400
We only have eight or 10 people.

613
00:41:25,401 --> 00:41:28,480
Yeah, and it's hard to A, find these people,

614
00:41:28,481 --> 00:41:30,280
and B, infiltrate them.

615
00:41:30,281 --> 00:41:33,080
It used to be the slogan was don't sweat the small stuff.

616
00:41:33,081 --> 00:41:34,880
Now we've got to sweat the small stuff.

617
00:41:34,881 --> 00:41:36,580
It's where the challenge will be.

618
00:41:39,320 --> 00:41:42,700
The task of identifying threats to our national security

619
00:41:42,701 --> 00:41:46,660
has prompted the use of new technologies.

620
00:41:46,661 --> 00:41:50,140
Forward-looking infrared is one such system.

621
00:41:50,141 --> 00:41:52,840
It is capable of detecting emanations of heat

622
00:41:52,841 --> 00:41:53,740
solid objects.

623
00:41:56,940 --> 00:42:00,820
Critics point out that FLIR, like other domestic surveillance

624
00:42:00,821 --> 00:42:04,620
technologies, was first developed for use on the battlefield.

625
00:42:10,020 --> 00:42:12,520
Suddenly, the same technology that

626
00:42:12,521 --> 00:42:17,460
was used to track soldiers at 1,000 meters in the dark

627
00:42:17,461 --> 00:42:21,760
can also be used in a city to follow individuals moving

628
00:42:21,761 --> 00:42:23,520
at 1,000 meters of the top.

629
00:42:23,521 --> 00:42:26,300
It looks like we have an officer on the tracks

630
00:42:26,301 --> 00:42:27,300
with the subject.

631
00:42:27,301 --> 00:42:29,640
Law enforcement agencies routinely

632
00:42:29,641 --> 00:42:34,140
use FLIR technology to hunt down suspected criminals.

633
00:42:34,141 --> 00:42:35,800
The Treasury Building.

634
00:42:35,801 --> 00:42:37,440
As the director of the FBI has said,

635
00:42:37,441 --> 00:42:40,820
we need to do these things to protect national security,

636
00:42:40,821 --> 00:42:43,820
to reduce crime, to protect public safety.

637
00:42:43,821 --> 00:42:45,920
It's very hard to argue with that.

638
00:42:45,921 --> 00:42:50,280
These are goals that are widely shared by a lot of people.

639
00:42:50,281 --> 00:42:53,780
The problem is if we accept that open-ended rationale

640
00:42:53,880 --> 00:42:56,820
that anything can be done to protect national security,

641
00:42:56,920 --> 00:42:58,980
anything can be done to reduce crime,

642
00:42:59,080 --> 00:43:02,720
protect public safety, there's really no stopping point.

643
00:43:02,820 --> 00:43:07,040
Every new surveillance technique, every new way to monitor,

644
00:43:07,140 --> 00:43:08,440
gets a green light.

645
00:43:08,540 --> 00:43:10,740
And that's the problem we face today.

646
00:43:14,040 --> 00:43:15,940
Private citizens and corporations

647
00:43:16,040 --> 00:43:20,280
have become so convinced of the need to spy on one another

648
00:43:20,281 --> 00:43:25,160
that video cameras small enough to mount in a thermostat,

649
00:43:25,161 --> 00:43:30,180
land in a cell phone, or conceal in a tie sell

650
00:43:30,181 --> 00:43:31,320
on a routine basis.

651
00:43:34,060 --> 00:43:37,000
Even high ticket items are popular.

652
00:43:37,001 --> 00:43:40,340
The Oscar, an omni-spectral correlator

653
00:43:40,341 --> 00:43:44,660
capable of tuning into any frequency,

654
00:43:44,661 --> 00:43:47,560
and ideal for sweeping a room for video cameras

655
00:43:47,561 --> 00:43:54,460
and bugging devices, or the global positioning system

656
00:43:54,461 --> 00:43:57,540
designed to attach to the bumper of a car

657
00:43:57,541 --> 00:44:01,040
and capable of tracking an individual anywhere in the world.

658
00:44:05,120 --> 00:44:09,880
The widespread use of such spy devices concerns experts.

659
00:44:09,881 --> 00:44:12,980
They assert that the lines between legitimate surveillance

660
00:44:12,981 --> 00:44:19,520
techniques and police state tactics have become blurred.

661
00:44:19,620 --> 00:44:21,920
That's really the big issue here.

662
00:44:22,020 --> 00:44:24,260
Where do we draw some lines?

663
00:44:24,360 --> 00:44:26,420
We recognize the need for public safety.

664
00:44:26,520 --> 00:44:29,600
We recognize the need for national security.

665
00:44:29,700 --> 00:44:31,460
But if we don't draw some lines, if we

666
00:44:31,560 --> 00:44:34,460
don't say that certain types of surveillance simply

667
00:44:34,560 --> 00:44:37,440
shouldn't be permitted, then we'll very quickly see,

668
00:44:37,540 --> 00:44:41,680
I think, that we have no privacy.

669
00:44:41,681 --> 00:44:44,660
Critics warn that if high-tech surveillance continues

670
00:44:44,661 --> 00:44:49,180
to outstrip mankind's ability to control its applications,

671
00:44:49,181 --> 00:44:53,800
privacy will soon be only a distant memory,

672
00:44:53,801 --> 00:44:57,720
and spying will take on a life of its own.

